look out for that, too!..

**Let's Dive Right In! We'd like to Introduce you to our New
Readability Feature! **

I'm sure that given your experience with online content you have
likely heard of Readability. You've probably even used some of the off
the shelf content marketing tools such as Grammarly and Hemingway etc..

... but did you know that the commonly recommended Readability
Metrics are outdated? 

Don't worry- we didn't either until we did a MASSIVE study that
examined the top 20 webpages for 1,000 popular keywords to find out if
Readability Score influenced Google Search rank.

The results were pretty crazy and shed some light on some interesting
findings! 

You can read the full study here:
www.originality.ai/blog/best-readability-score-to-rank-in-google
<https://jonhaver.lt.acemlnb.com/Prod/link-tracker?redirectUrl=aHR0cCUzQSUyRiUyRnd3dy5vcmlnaW5hbGl0eS5haSUyRmJsb2clMkZiZXN0LXJlYWRhYmlsaXR5LXNjb3JlLXRvLXJhbmstaW4tZ29vZ2xl&sig=7uhRxy813KBeRdjPNFJeEbELBu36tXi2nYdBSiMTuQAt&iat=1686060642&a=%7C%7C798597961%7C%7C&account=jonhaver%2Eactivehosted%2Ecom&email=Uoy7oyhzDWMtOUKXBnrWZUdN4HS1wFiP19idGz5nDAtWNY5QDVqhfoUHVZI%3D%3A5khZsxQoXoaFpVyctuHvuJa2%2FXfKBuAi&s=c2aa77b6f0a29bb9a1c1a13f88329783&i=1852A2018A61A28722>

**Here is a Quick Synopsis of our Case Study Findings: **

* Although it is commonly accepted that content should target readers at
a grade 10 education level, the data consistently revealed that the mean
readability grade is grade 12 or higher in nearly every case, which also
correlates with the top ranked pages on Google

* The findings of the study suggest avoiding readability systems like
SMOG, Coleman-Liau, Flesch-Kincaid Grade, and Automated Readability
systems, as their results showed a very high rate of skewed data
(results were not normally distributed).

(Awkward...given some of the most popular tools use some these
Readability systems..) 

via GIPHY <https://jonhaver.lt.acemlnb.com/Prod/link-tracker?redirectUrl=aHR0cHMlM0ElMkYlMkZnaXBoeS5jb20lMkZnaWZzJTJGdGhlb2ZmaWNlLTRaTXNYRzF1aDZjWlRvMUY5WQ==&sig=CLdbckskXTLHxSNUkBFBQmgwhw4mWebUWvXAhMw6yMfC&iat=1686060642&a=%7C%7C798597961%7C%7C&account=jonhaver%2Eactivehosted%2Ecom&email=Uoy7oyhzDWMtOUKXBnrWZUdN4HS1wFiP19idGz5nDAtWNY5QDVqhfoUHVZI%3D%3A5khZsxQoXoaFpVyctuHvuJa2%2FXfKBuAi&s=c2aa77b6f0a29bb9a1c1a13f88329783&i=1852A2018A61A28723>

* Often quoted target readability scores were shown to be lower than the
study would suggest.

* Our findings suggest that the top results in Google have the following
readability scores:

How can the previous well-established and widely accepted readability
metrics be inaccurate and just plain wrong in determining the ideal
scores for online content with relation to their ranking capabilities? 

One possible explanation is that these readability systems, originally
developed between the 1940s and 1970s, may no longer accurately
represent current literacy levels. IE they are outdated and no longer
accurate. 

Alternatively, it could be that the internet audience is not as diverse
as the global population and may self-select readers with above-average
reading abilities.

**Regardless of the explanation.... Our Readability Feature was built to
guide you with Readability Scores that are congruent with what is
actually ranking on Google Search Engines **

